#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tsx-bench-compare: compare the result files of tsx-bench (host side).

usage: tsx-bench-compare [--md FILE] [--html FILE] [--title TEXT] [--no-text]
                         RESULT.json [RESULT.json ...]
       tsx-bench-compare --rows

The tool reads the JSON files that `tsx-bench` wrote (schema tsx-bench/1) and
prints a Markdown report on stdout.

Every test row has a score. The score of a row is a rate in a fixed unit. A
rate (MiB/s, MB/s, MFLOPS, Ksteps/s, IOPS, fps, chunks/s) is the score as it
is. A time in seconds becomes runs per second (1 / seconds). A time in
milliseconds becomes events per second (1000 / ms). A latency in ns becomes
accesses per microsecond (1000 / ns). The unit of each row is in the report.

The score of a group is the geometric mean of its row scores. The overall score
is the geometric mean of the six group scores. Only a panel that has every group
gets an overall score. The core score is the geometric mean of CPU, Memory,
Sustained load and Storage, so a panel without a browser test still compares.
The sustained-load ratio (last 10 s / first 10 s) is shown and not scored.

No panel is the reference and no score is scaled. The unit of the number is the
geometric-mean unit. The ratio of two scores does not depend on the units of
the rows. A new unit or a new set of rows is a new score version.

The tool computes the scores from the values. A file without scores (made by an
older tsx-bench) works the same. The table of rows is in this file and in
tsx-bench-core.c. The tests make sure that both are equal.

Network and temperature are shown and not scored.

The Comparison section puts the panels side by side. A cell shows the score,
the percentage of the best panel in the row and the factor of the best panel
(best score divided by the score of the panel).

--md FILE    also write the report to FILE
--html FILE  write one self-contained HTML page (no script, no network)
--title TEXT title of the report (default "Panel benchmark")
--rows       print the table of scored rows and exit

Python 3, standard library only.
"""
import argparse
import html
import json
import math
import re
import sys

SCHEMA = "tsx-bench/1"
SCORE_VERSION = 2

HIGH, LOW = "high", "low"

